---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ENTITY_STATEMENT } from '../data/site.js';
import prompt from '../../public/own-your-name-prompt.md?raw';
---

<Base
  title="Own your name"
  path="/own-your-name"
  description={`${ENTITY_STATEMENT} How he built this site, and a copy-paste prompt so you can build your own canonical home for humans and AI.`}
>
  <h1>Own your name</h1>
  <p class="kicker">Become the authoritative source about yourself, for people and for AI. Here's how, and here's help.</p>

  <p class="lede">
    Search your own name and count the versions of you. A stale job title on a data broker. A
    speaker bio that 404s. A cached headline from two employers ago. When an AI assistant is asked
    who you are, it has to arbitrate between all of them, and it will pick wrong. I know because I
    had exactly that problem, and this site is how I fixed it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The fix is a site you control that states who you are once, clearly, in a form both humans and
    machines can read: a canonical entity statement repeated consistently, structured data on
    every page, an llms.txt for AI crawlers, a machine-readable resume, and a robots.txt that says
    yes to being read. Then every profile you own points here, and the fragments collapse into one
    person. This whole site was built in a sitting with Claude, deployed free, and it updates
    whenever I tell it what changed.
  </p>

  <h2>I'll help you do it</h2>
  <p>
    I like this work. If you want a hand getting your own site up, or you've built one and want a
    second set of eyes on how AI systems are reading it, email
    <a href="mailto:ally@allymadrone.com">ally@allymadrone.com</a>. No pitch, no fee. This is the
    same thing I do for a living at a much larger scale, and I think everyone should own the
    answer to "who is this person."
  </p>

  <h2>Or do it yourself with this prompt</h2>
  <p>
    Below is the prompt that produces a site like this one. Paste it into Claude, attach your
    resume, and answer the questions it asks. It works best in Claude Code, which can build,
    deploy, and connect your domain. In a regular chat it will still interview you and draft every
    page for you to publish yourself. Have three things ready first: a resume, a headshot, and a
    domain you own. You can also
    <a href="/own-your-name-prompt.md" download>download the prompt as a text file</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="prompt-tools">
    <button type="button" id="copy-prompt" class="copy-btn" hidden>Copy prompt</button>
  </div>
  <pre class="prompt" id="prompt-text" tabindex="0" set:text={prompt}></pre>

  <h2>What to expect</h2>
  <p>
    Claude interviews you in small rounds, then drafts every page for you to redline before it
    builds anything. The questions about privacy are the ones people skip and regret: decide up
    front what never goes on the site, and the prompt makes Claude hold that line everywhere,
    including the machine-readable layers you'll never look at. You'll end up with a fast site at
    your own domain, automatic HTTPS, free hosting, and a workflow where updating a page means
    describing the change.
  </p>

  <script>
    const btn = document.getElementById('copy-prompt');
    const pre = document.getElementById('prompt-text');
    if (btn && pre && navigator.clipboard) {
      btn.hidden = false;
      const label = btn.textContent;
      btn.addEventListener('click', async () => {
        try {
          await navigator.clipboard.writeText(pre.textContent || '');
          btn.textContent = 'Copied';
          setTimeout(() => (btn.textContent = label), 2000);
        } catch {
          btn.textContent = 'Select the text and copy';
        }
      });
    }
  </script>
</Base>
